  "response": "I bounded the fan-out in `test/utils.ts` so at most 16 file writes are in flight at once. I couldn't run the test suite because the working copy has no `node_modules`, and my standalone `tsc` check gave no clear result.\n\n## The finding\n\n**crime_00024 — `unbounded_async_fanout` (\"Concurrency Stampede\")**, `test/utils.ts:38-46`, symbol `run → mkdir`.\n\nEvidence for the fan-out site and the collection it iterates:\n- `\"fan-out: Promise.all at line 38\"`\n- `\"collection: Object.keys(files).map(…)\"`\n- `\"produced by: Object.keys\"`\n- `\"per-element work (2):\"` / `\"filesystem operation — fsp.mkdir at line 43\"` / `\"filesystem operation — fsp.writeFile at line 44\"`\n- `\"no bound visible: no .slice() or take/limit on the source, no batching, no concurrency-limit helper, no semaphore\"`\n- `\"if the collection holds N elements, N concurrent filesystem operation start at once — N is a property of the data, not of this code\"`\n\nOne part of the evidence is wrong: `\"collection source: an awaited call (query, request, or listing)\"`. `files` is the fixture object passed in by each test, not an awaited call. That doesn't change the diagnosis, because the size is still set by the data and not by the helper.\n\n## Why it fails on CI but not locally\n\n`run()` starts a `mkdir` and a `writeFile` for every fixture file at the same moment. Each `writeFile` holds a file descriptor (open, write, close) until it finishes. Peak descriptor use is therefore roughly the number of fixture files, plus whatever else the process has open. Whether that passes the per-process limit (`ulimit -n`) depends on three things that have nothing to do with the code:\n\n1. **The descriptor limit.** macOS dev shells are often raised to 10k or more, or \"unlimited\", by shell config or launchd. CI containers often keep a soft limit of 1024, or lower in some images.\n2. **How long each write stays open.** On a fast local SSD with an idle disk, writes finish almost immediately, so few are open at the same moment. On a busy CI runner with shared, slower storage, they stay open longer and more pile up.\n3. **Other work in the same process.** Vitest runs test files in parallel workers, and other tests in the same worker may already hold descriptors. That headroom changes with scheduling on each run.\n\nSo the same fixture can stay under the limit on a laptop and go over it on CI, and only on some runs. That makes it intermittent and dependent on the machine.\n\n## The fix (`test/utils.ts`)\n\nThe write loop is now its own helper, `writeFixtureFiles`, which runs a fixed pool of workers:\n\n```ts\nconst WRITE_CONCURRENCY = 16\n\n// Write fixture files with a fixed number of writes in flight, so a large\n// fixture can't exhaust the process's file descriptors (EMFILE).\nasync function writeFixtureFiles(\n  testDir: string,\n  files: { [name: string]: string },\n) {\n  const names = Object.keys(files)\n  let next = 0\n  const writeNext = async (): Promise<void> => {\n    while (next < names.length) {\n      const name = names[next++]\n      const filePath = path.resolve(testDir, name)\n      const parentDir = path.dirname(filePath)\n      // Thanks to `recursive: true`, this doesn't fail even if the directory already exists.\n      await fsp.mkdir(parentDir, { recursive: true })\n      await fsp.writeFile(filePath, files[name], 'utf8')\n    }\n  }\n  await Promise.all(\n    Array.from(\n      { length: Math.min(WRITE_CONCURRENCY, names.length) },\n      writeNext,\n    ),\n  )\n}\n```\n\n`run()` now just calls `await writeFixtureFiles(testDir, files)`.\n\n- **Why a pool:** `next++` happens synchronously between awaits, so no two workers can take the same file. Peak descriptor use is now at most 16, whatever the size of the fixture. The behaviour is otherwise the same: every file is written, and the first error still rejects `run()`.\n- **Why not batches:** chunked `Promise.all` waits for the slowest write in each chunk. A pool starts the next file as soon as any slot is free.\n- **Why no library:** the project has no `p-limit` or similar dependency, so I didn't add one just for a test helper.\n- **Why a separate function:** inlining the pool pushed `run` to 65 lines, which set off the scanner's God Function rule (60-line limit). Moving it into a helper keeps `run` under the limit. After the edit, the scan no longer reported the fan-out.\n\n## Verification\n- **Tests:** not run, because there is no `node_modules`.\n- **Type check:** inconclusive. I ran `npx tsc` on the file by itself, and npx resolved through the project's `tsup` package. It printed no errors, but I'm not sure it actually type-checked the file.\n\nTo check the fix: install dependencies and run `vitest` under `ulimit -n 256`, with a fixture of several hundred files. Without the fix that should reproduce EMFILE; with it, it should pass.\n\nThere's no git repository here, so nothing was committed.",
